steps.body:
Every C++ program starts at `int main()`. The line `#include <iostream>` pastes in the declarations of the standard input and output streams, including `std::cout`, the console output. `return 0;` at the end of `main` tells the system the program succeeded. Every statement ends with a semicolon.

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::cout << "Hello" << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

The `<<` operator sends a value into the stream, and you can chain several `<<` in one statement. Text in double quotes, like `"Hello"`, is a string literal. One character in single quotes, like `'A'`, is a char literal. `'\n'` is also a single char: the newline. Leave it out and the next output continues on the same line.

Do not mix the quotes up. Single quotes hold exactly one character, so use double quotes for words. `'Hello'` is not text: g++ only warns and prints a strange number instead.

`std::` says the name lives in the standard library's namespace. This course always writes it instead of `using namespace std;`, which pulls every standard name into your program where it can clash with your own names such as `count` or `max`.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::cout << "Welcome to Hack University" << '\n';
    std::cout << "Lesson 1: Printing" << '\n';
    std::cout << "Let's write C++!" << '\n';
    return 0;
}

